N2  - View Video Presentation: https://doi.org/10.2514/6.2022-4207.vidAs the space industry expands to include start-up companies and emerging space nations, there is pressing need for technical workforce development. Paper-based ?book learning? only takes us so far. Real hardware and software behave differently than models predict. The EssentialSAT desktop space system was developed to address this challenge. This low-cost, desktop satellite simulator system provides a unique, hands-on learning experience as a part of an integrated space systems engineering curriculum. EssentialSAT provides an educational kit of representative spacecraft subsystems that can be assembled, integrated, and tested in the classroom with simple ground support equipment. The discussion of EssentialSAT development highlights the application of an integrated digital ecosystem that includes CAD, eCAD, addictive manufacturing and all orchestrated using model-based systems engineering (MBSE). Its use as part of a classroom experience that allows students to learn about individual spacecraft subsystems?power, data handling, communication, attitude determination and control, remote sensing, mechanisms?as well as the assembly, integration and verification and operation of the entire system. The paper describes details of the system elements and interfaces. Conclusions describe describe future applications and its potential available as an open-source design.
